.TH FAIL2ZIG-CLIENT 1 "2026-09-09" "fail2zig 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
fail2zig\-client \- query and control the fail2zig intrusion prevention daemon
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fail2zig\-client
[\fIglobal\-flags\fR]
.I command
[\fIcommand\-args\fR]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fail2zig\-client
is the command-line interface to the
.BR fail2zig (1)
daemon. It communicates over the daemon's Unix domain socket using a
length-prefixed binary protocol.
.PP
The client requires either root privileges (uid=0) or membership in the
.B fail2zig
group to connect. If the daemon is not running, all commands exit with code 3
and print a diagnostic message indicating the reason (connection refused,
permission denied, or timeout).
.PP
Output format is configurable: tabular (the default, with ANSI color on a
terminal), JSON, or plain tab-separated. Use
.B \-\-output json
for scripting.
.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
Global flags may appear before or after the command name.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Unix socket path. Default:
.IR /run/fail2zig/fail2zig.sock .
Override when the daemon is configured with a non-default
.B global.socket_path
or when testing against a non-production daemon instance.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " format"
Output format:
.BR table " (default), " json ", or " plain .
.B table
renders box-drawing borders with ANSI color on a terminal and without on
non-terminals.
.B plain
emits tab-separated values with no borders or color, suitable for
.BR awk (1)
or
.BR cut (1)
processing.
.B json
emits compact JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI color output unconditionally. Equivalent to setting a non-tty output.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " ms"
Command timeout in milliseconds. Default: 5000 (5 seconds). If the daemon does
not respond within this window, the client exits with code 3.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the client version and exit. Does not contact the daemon.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage information and exit.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS status
Show a summary of daemon health: uptime, memory usage, parse rate, active bans,
total bans, protection state, firewall backend, and jail count.
.PP
The
.B Protection
row is one of
.B active
(every enabled jail enforces),
.B log-only
(no jail enforces),
.B mixed
(some jails enforce, some observe), or
.B DEGRADED (\fIcause\fB)
(an enforcing jail cannot be honoured: no usable firewall backend, or its log
source is broken). The daemon never runs degraded silently; the same state is
exported on
.B /metrics
and
.BR /events .
.B Backend
is
.B none
when no firewall backend is in use. With
.B \-\-output json
the raw
.B protection
and
.B protection_cause
fields are emitted.
.PP
.nf
.B "$ sudo fail2zig-client status"
+------------------------------------------+
| fail2zig 0.3.0 — running                |
+------------------------------------------+
| Uptime:      0d 0h 40m 54s               |
| Memory:      0.0 MB                      |
| Parse rate:  0 lines/sec                 |
| Active bans: 0                           |
| Total bans:  0                           |
| Protection:  active                      |
| Backend:     nftables                    |
| Jails:       2                           |
+------------------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
.SS ban \fIip\fR \fB\-\-jail\fR \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-duration\fR \fIseconds\fR]
Manually ban an IP address.
.B \-\-jail
is required and names the jail the ban is attributed to (per-jail accounting,
and the target for
.BR unban ).
The optional
.B \-\-duration
flag sets the ban duration in seconds; if omitted, the jail's resolved
.B bantime
is used (its override or the global default). Repeated requests extend existing
ownership without incrementing the ban counter again.
.PP
Requires a known, enabled, enforcing jail and an address outside its ignore list.
Requests that cannot be enforced return an error; failed firewall applications
remain tracked for retry and make protection degraded.
.PP
.nf
.B "$ sudo fail2zig-client ban 45.227.253.98 --jail sshd"
banned: 45.227.253.98 (jail=sshd, duration=3600s)
.fi
.PP
.SS unban \fIip\fR [\fB\-\-jail\fR \fIname\fR]
Remove a ban. If
.B \-\-jail
is specified, only the ban in that jail is removed. If omitted, the ban is
removed from all jails. Other jail ownership remains enforced when only one jail
is cleared. Log-only tracked bans can be cleared without a firewall backend.
Removal failures return an error and retain tracked state.
.PP
.nf
.B "$ sudo fail2zig-client unban 45.227.253.98 --jail sshd"
unbanned: 45.227.253.98
.fi
.PP
.SS list [\fB\-\-jail\fR \fIname\fR]
List active bans. Each row shows the IP, the jail it was banned by, the time
remaining, and how many times that IP has been banned. Without
.BR \-\-jail ,
the list comes from the daemon's own state and works with or without a
firewall backend. With
.BR \-\-jail ,
the daemon reads that jail's set back from the firewall backend; without one
it answers
.B "no firewall backend"
and the client exits 1.
.PP
.nf
.B "$ sudo fail2zig-client list"
IP ADDRESS      JAIL  TIME LEFT  BAN COUNT
------------------------------------------
45.227.253.98   sshd  57m 43s    1
Total: 1 active bans
.fi
.PP
When nothing is banned the table output is the single line
.BR "No active bans." ;
JSON output is an empty array.
.PP
.SS jails
List all configured jails with their state, active ban count, thresholds,
effective action, and log source.
.B ACTION
is the backend the jail resolves to at runtime
.RB ( nftables ", " iptables ", " ipset ", or " log-only )
and
.B ENFORCING
is
.B true
only when bans actually reach the firewall.
.B SOURCE
is the log path (or comma-separated paths),
.BI "journald (" filter )
for the systemd journal, or
.B internal
for jails fed in-process from other jails' confirmed bans (the
.B recidive
filter).
.B HEALTH
is
.B ok
when the source was last read successfully,
.B broken
when the daemon can no longer read it (this also marks the daemon
.BR DEGRADED ),
or
.B unknown
before the first read. An
.B internal
source is always
.BR ok .
.PP
.nf
.B "$ sudo fail2zig-client jails"
JAIL      STATE    ACTIVE  MAX RETRY  FIND TIME  BAN TIME  ACTION    ENFORCING  SOURCE           HEALTH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
sshd      enabled  0       3          10m        5m        nftables  true       journald (sshd)  ok
recidive  enabled  0       3          1d         7d        nftables  true       internal         ok
Total: 2 jails
.fi
.PP
.SS reload
Not implemented. Returns an error (IPC status 501); no configuration is changed.
Validate the new configuration and restart the service to apply it.
.PP
.SS version
Print the daemon version (requires a daemon connection, unlike
.BR \-\-version ).
.PP
.nf
.B "$ sudo fail2zig-client version"
fail2zig-client 0.3.0
.fi
.PP
.SS completions \fIshell\fR
Print a shell completion script for the specified shell to stdout.
Supported shells:
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish .
.PP
.nf
.B "$ fail2zig-client completions bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/fail2zig-client"
.B "$ fail2zig-client completions zsh  > ~/.zsh/completions/_fail2zig-client"
.B "$ fail2zig-client completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/fail2zig-client.fish"
.fi
.PP
.SS help [\fIcommand\fR]
Print general help or help for a specific command.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/fail2zig/fail2zig.sock
Default Unix socket path. The socket is created by
.BR fail2zig (1)
at startup with mode 0660. Override with
.BR \-\-socket .
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The daemon returned an error response (e.g., unknown jail name, IP not found).
.TP
.B 2
Bad arguments or invalid input (unknown command, missing required argument,
unrecognized IP address format).
.TP
.B 3
Connection failed: daemon is not running, permission denied, or command timed out.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
No environment variables are read by the client.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Check daemon health
.nf
sudo fail2zig-client status
.fi
.SS Ban an IP manually for 24 hours
.nf
sudo fail2zig-client ban 192.0.2.10 --jail sshd --duration 86400
.fi
.SS List active bans as JSON for scripting
.nf
sudo fail2zig-client list --output json | jq '.[].ip'
.fi
.SS Watch for bans in a loop
.nf
watch -n 5 'sudo fail2zig-client list --output plain'
.fi
.SS Install bash completions system-wide
.nf
sudo fail2zig-client completions bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/fail2zig-client
.fi
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fail2zig (1),
.BR fail2zig.toml (5)
.
